// THE COLUMN DECLARATION SITE — an `Option<i64>` COLUMN IS REFUSED, AND WHAT
// THIS FIXTURE PINS IS THE DIAGNOSTIC, NOT THE REFUSAL.
//
// ⚠ WHAT THIS PROGRAM DID BEFORE THE REFUSAL EXISTED, MEASURED ON THIS EXACT
// SOURCE. It COMPILED (rc 0), RAN (rc 0), and `group by s.o` answered TWO
// groups over four rows carrying THREE distinct values, while the `i64` control
// column `c` in the SAME struct and the SAME program answered three. Nothing
// was reported at any layer. That is the whole ground for refusing here: the
// cost of the old behaviour was not a bad build, it was a wrong answer with no
// symptom.
//
// THE MECHANISM. `el::el_ty_of_name` launders every type name outside the EL
// lattice to `EL_TY_INT`, and the key vector the group-by emitter builds is a
// `Vec<i64>`. So the eight bytes read for the key are the first eight of the
// value — and the MLIR type the verifier printed for this column is
// `!llvm.struct<"enum.Option__i64", (i32, array<1 x i64>)>`, whose first eight
// bytes are the DISCRIMINANT. Hence exactly one group per VARIANT: `Some` and
// `None`, two groups, regardless of the payloads.
//
// ⚠ WHY NOT AT `el_ty_of_name`. Only a NAME reaches that function, and its
// leniency is load-bearing there: an unknown field name is a nested struct the
// EL never reads a scalar from, or a generic alias to its own parameter
// (`type El<T> = T`, pinned by wql_key_spelling_e2e). Refusing there would
// refuse those. The type NODE is in hand one layer up, at the source's own
// field list, and that is where this fires — `reflect::column_decl_admit`.
//
// ⚠ THE REFUSAL IS ABOUT THE SOURCE'S DECLARATION, NOT ABOUT THIS CLAUSE. A
// query that binds this struct and never mentions `o` is refused too. That is
// the stated cost of the declaration-site variant and it is deliberate: the
// column's type is known there and nowhere earlier.
package test;

use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

struct S { pub o: Option<i64>, pub c: i64, pub id: i64 }

pub deem qo(ss: &[S]) { from ss s group by s.o aggregate n = count() select n }

fn main() -> i32 { return 0i32; }
